## VPC Fundamentals

### 1. VPC Creation

```bash
# Create VPC with CIDR block
aws ec2 create-vpc \
  --cidr-block 10.0.0.0/16 \
  --tag-specifications 'ResourceType=vpc,Tags=[{Key=Name,Value=production-vpc}]'

# Output:
# VPC ID: vpc-12345678
# CIDR Block: 10.0.0.0/16
# State: Available

# Enable DNS hostnames
aws ec2 modify-vpc-attribute \
  --vpc-id vpc-12345678 \
  --enable-dns-hostnames

# Enable DNS resolution
aws ec2 modify-vpc-attribute \
  --vpc-id vpc-12345678 \
  --enable-dns-support

# Describe VPC
aws ec2 describe-vpcs \
  --vpc-ids vpc-12345678
```

### 2. CIDR Block Breakdown

```
CIDR Notation: 10.0.0.0/16

IP Format:
- 10.0.0.0 = Network address
- /16 = Prefix length (number of bits for network)
  - Remaining bits (32-16=16) for host addresses
  - Total IPs: 2^16 = 65,536
  - Usable IPs: 65,536 - 5 (AWS reserved) = 65,531

AWS Reserved IPs in each CIDR:
- .0 = Network address
- .1 = VPC router
- .2 = DNS
- .3 = Future use
- .255 = Broadcast

Example for 10.0.0.0/16:
- Total: 65,536 IPs
- Reserved: 5 IPs per subnet
- Usable: 65,531 IPs

Common CIDR Blocks:
- 10.0.0.0/8 (large: 16.7M IPs)
- 10.0.0.0/16 (medium: 65K IPs)
- 10.0.0.0/24 (small: 256 IPs)
- 172.16.0.0/12 (private range)
- 192.168.0.0/16 (private range)
```

### 3. VPC Endpoints vs NAT Gateway

| Aspect | VPC Endpoint | NAT Gateway |
|--------|-------------|------------|
| Purpose | AWS service access | Internet access |
| Cost | Free or $7.20/month | $32/month + data |
| Location | Within VPC | In public subnet |
| Traffic | Private | Through NAT |
| Services | S3, DynamoDB, SNS | Internet (HTTP/HTTPS) |
| HA | Redundant by default | Single point |

