## NAT Gateway

### 1. NAT Gateway Setup

```bash
# Step 1: Create Elastic IP in public subnet
EIP=$(aws ec2 allocate-address \
  --domain vpc \
  --tag-specifications 'ResourceType=elastic-ip,Tags=[{Key=Name,Value=nat-eip}]' \
  --query 'AllocationId' \
  --output text)

echo "Elastic IP: $EIP"

# Step 2: Create NAT Gateway (MUST be in public subnet)
NAT_GW=$(aws ec2 create-nat-gateway \
  --subnet-id subnet-public-1a \
  --allocation-id $EIP \
  --tag-specifications 'ResourceType=nat-gateway,Tags=[{Key=Name,Value=nat-prod}]' \
  --query 'NatGateway.NatGatewayId' \
  --output text)

echo "NAT Gateway: $NAT_GW"

# Wait for NAT Gateway to be available
aws ec2 wait nat-gateway-available \
  --nat-gateway-ids $NAT_GW

# Step 3: Create route in private subnet route table
aws ec2 create-route \
  --route-table-id rtb-private \
  --destination-cidr-block 0.0.0.0/0 \
  --nat-gateway-id $NAT_GW

# Verify
aws ec2 describe-nat-gateways \
  --nat-gateway-ids $NAT_GW

# Monitor NAT Gateway (watch data transfer)
aws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics \
  --namespace AWS/NatGateway \
  --metric-name BytesOutToDestination \
  --dimensions Name=NatGatewayId,Value=$NAT_GW \
  --statistics Sum \
  --start-time 2025-11-25T00:00:00Z \
  --end-time 2025-11-26T00:00:00Z \
  --period 3600
```

### 2. NAT Gateway High Availability

```bash
# Create NAT Gateway in each AZ for HA

# AZ 1a: Public Subnet
EIP_1A=$(aws ec2 allocate-address --domain vpc --query 'AllocationId' --output text)
NAT_1A=$(aws ec2 create-nat-gateway \
  --subnet-id subnet-public-1a \
  --allocation-id $EIP_1A \
  --query 'NatGateway.NatGatewayId' \
  --output text)

# AZ 1b: Public Subnet
EIP_1B=$(aws ec2 allocate-address --domain vpc --query 'AllocationId' --output text)
NAT_1B=$(aws ec2 create-nat-gateway \
  --subnet-id subnet-public-1b \
  --allocation-id $EIP_1B \
  --query 'NatGateway.NatGatewayId' \
  --output text)

# Private subnet 1a: Route to NAT 1a
aws ec2 create-route \
  --route-table-id rtb-private-1a \
  --destination-cidr-block 0.0.0.0/0 \
  --nat-gateway-id $NAT_1A

# Private subnet 1b: Route to NAT 1b
aws ec2 create-route \
  --route-table-id rtb-private-1b \
  --destination-cidr-block 0.0.0.0/0 \
  --nat-gateway-id $NAT_1B

# Benefits:
# - If NAT 1a fails, 1a subnet traffic still routes locally
# - Better data transfer (inter-AZ cheaper than NAT)
# - Better throughput (each NAT handles own AZ)
```

### 3. NAT Gateway vs NAT Instance

| Aspect | NAT Gateway | NAT Instance |
|--------|------------|--------------|
| AWS-managed | Yes | No (you manage EC2) |
| HA | Automatic (redundant) | Manual (Multi-AZ) |
| Bandwidth | Up to 100 Gbps | Limited by EC2 type |
| Cost | $32/month + data | EC2 instance cost |
| Maintenance | None | You maintain |
| Failover | Automatic | Manual |
| Security Group | N/A | You configure |
| Performance | Better | Less predictable |

